3. Layer 1 — Application allocation

            Imagine:                    
                    @PostMapping("/orders")
                    public OrderResponse createOrder(OrderRequest request) {                    
                        Order order = orderService.createOrder(request);                    
                        return OrderResponse.from(order);
                    }

This tiny method may indirectly create hundreds or thousands of objects.

For example:
                OrderRequest
                ValidationResult
                String objects
                Collections
                Database entities
                Hibernate/JDBC objects
                Order
                OrderItems
                OrderResponse
                Jackson serialization objects
                temporary intermediate objects

Some objects may live for milliseconds.

Others may live for minutes, hours, or the lifetime of the application.

This difference in object lifetime is fundamental to GC design.
===============================================================================================================================================
4. Layer 2 — TLAB:

        Suppose several Java threads are simultaneously processing requests.
            
    If every allocation required synchronization:
                                                Thread A ─┐
                                                Thread B ─┼──> shared allocation area
                                                Thread C ─┘
    allocation would become expensive.
    
    Instead, HotSpot commonly gives each Java thread a small allocation buffer:

        Thread A → TLAB A
        Thread B → TLAB B
        Thread C → TLAB C

Then:
        new Order()
             ↓
        Thread-local allocation
             ↓
        TLAB
             ↓
        Eden region

This makes the extremely common operation of allocating small objects very fast.

===============================================================================================================================================
5. Layer 3 — G1 regions

            This is where G1 differs conceptually from older contiguous-generation designs.
            
            Think of the heap as:
                                +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+
                                | Eden | Eden | Old  | Eden | Surv | Free | Old |
                                +------+------+------+------+------+------+------+

            These are logical roles of regions.
            
            A region is a fixed-size chunk of heap memory.
            
            G1 can dynamically assign region roles.
            
            So you should avoid saying:            
                    "The first 40% of the heap is young and the remaining 60% is old."
            
            That is not the right mental model for G1.
            
            Instead:            
                    The heap is a collection of regions whose roles can change over time.
            
            That is a very good L6 interview distinction.
===============================================================================================================================================
6. Layer 4 — Why does Young GC happen?

Suppose:
            Eden
            ────────────────────
            ████████████████████
            ████████████████████
            ████████████████████

    Most objects created by requests are temporary.    
    Eventually G1 needs more space for allocation.    
    It initiates a young collection.    
    The application threads are temporarily paused.
    
    This is why you will often hear:    
                        Young GC is a Stop-The-World evacuation pause.
    
    Do not say that G1 is completely pause-free.    
    It isn't.
    
    That's an important interview correction.

===============================================================================================================================================
7. Layer 5 — How does G1 know what is alive?

            This is the heart of garbage collection.
            
            Suppose we have:
                            GC Root
                               │
                               ▼
                            Object A
                               │
                               ▼
                            Object B

            A and B are reachable.
            
            Therefore:            
                        A → LIVE
                        B → LIVE

            But:           
                    Object X → Object Y
            
            has no connection to any GC root.
            
            Therefore:            
                        X → unreachable
                        Y → unreachable
            
            Those objects are garbage.
            
            The collector doesn't ask:            
                    "Did the developer call delete?"
            
            Java doesn't have manual object deletion.
            
            It asks:  "Can this object still be reached from the GC roots?"
===============================================================================================================================================
8. Layer 6 — The cross-region problem

            This is one of the most important parts for an L6 interview.
            
            Imagine:
                        Old Region
                        ┌──────────────┐
                        │ Object A     │
                        │      │       │
                        └──────┼───────┘
                               │
                               ▼
                        Eden Region
                        ┌──────────────┐
                        │ Object B     │
                        └──────────────┘

                Object A is in Old.                
                Object B is in Eden.                
                A points to B.
                
                Now G1 wants to collect Eden.                
                It cannot simply look only inside Eden because an Old object may point into Eden.                
                But scanning the entire Old generation during every young GC would be expensive.
                
                That's why G1 maintains remembered-set information.
===============================================================================================================================================
9. Card table → remembered set → write barrier

        These three concepts are frequently confused.        
        Think of them as different layers.
        
        Card table:        
                    The heap is divided conceptually into small card regions for tracking modifications.

                    Heap
                    ────────────────────────
                    | card | card | card |
                    | card | card | card |
                    ────────────────────────

                    A card can indicate that an area may contain a reference that needs attention.
                    
        Write barrier:
                    
                    When application code changes a reference:                    
                            oldObject.child = youngObject;
                    
                    the JVM can execute additional barrier logic around that reference update.
                    
                    Conceptually:
                                    Application write
                                           ↓
                                    Write barrier
                                           ↓
                                    Record relevant card/reference information

        Remembered set:
        
                    G1 maintains per-region information about references from elsewhere into that region.
                    
                    Conceptually:
                                    Region B
                                       ↑
                                       │
                                    Remembered Set
                                       │
                                       └── references from other regions

                    Therefore, during a Young GC, G1 can efficiently identify external references into the regions being collected.

        Excellent L6 phrasing:
                
                You can say:                
                            "Remembered sets allow G1 to avoid scanning the entire heap for inter-region 
                            references during every collection, 
                            while write barriers maintain the metadata needed to keep those remembered sets useful."
                
                That is much stronger than simply saying:                
                            "G1 uses remembered sets."

===============================================================================================================================================
10. Layer 7 — Evacuation

            Suppose Eden contains:
                                Eden Region:                                
                                            A → dead
                                            B → live
                                            C → dead
                                            D → live
                                            E → dead

G1 can conceptually transform this into:

                            Eden                 Survivor
                            
                            A ✕
                            B ──────────────────> B'
                            C ✕
                            D ──────────────────> D'
                            E ✕

Now:
        Old Eden Region
              ↓
        completely reclaimed

This is extremely powerful.

Instead of individually deleting:
                                    A
                                    C
                                    E

G1 evacuates the objects that matter and then reclaims the region.
This also helps with compaction.
===============================================================================================================================================
11. What about object references after copying?

            Suppose:            
                    A ──→ B
            
            and B gets moved:
                    
                    Old B address
                          ↓
                    New B address
            
            The JVM needs references to point to the object's new location.
            This is part of the machinery involved in evacuation and object forwarding.
            
            At the conceptual level:
                                    Old object
                                        │
                                        ▼
                                    Evacuated object
                                        │
                                        ▼
                                    References updated


            You don't need to claim a simplistic "forwarding pointer works exactly this way everywhere" 
            unless the interviewer asks for implementation-level source details, because HotSpot's precise mechanics can vary.


