18. Memory leak vs high allocation rate

    This is another excellent interview distinction.
    
    High allocation rate
    
        Objects:        
                    created
                     ↓
                    used
                     ↓
                    become unreachable
                     ↓
                    GC
                     ↓
                    reclaimed
This is not necessarily a memory leak.

Memory leak:
        Objects:
                    created
                     ↓
                    stored somewhere unnecessarily
                     ↓
                    remain reachable
                     ↓
                    GC cannot reclaim
                     ↓
                    Old generation grows
For example:
                static Map<String, Object> cache = new HashMap<>();
    
 If entries are continuously added without eviction:
            Request
              ↓
            Object
              ↓
            static Map
              ↓
            GC Root

The object remains reachable.

GC cannot say:
            "The developer probably doesn't need this anymore."

GC only knows reachability.
This is why GC cannot fix logical memory leaks.

